// EXPECT: currently-rejected
// Violates: docs/OWNERSHIP.md R10 (an `arc` place may not be passed to an
// `owned` parameter).
//
// Rejected with R10's own message, and this is the one clause of R10 that is
// implemented. It is a refusal rather than a retain because a retain cannot
// fix it. `owned [T]` and `shared [T]` are the SAME C type, `cell_slice_t` by
// value, so the C backend's unbox emitted
//
//     cell_take((*(const cell_slice_t *)xs.ptr));
//     cell_arc_drop(xs);
//
// which compiles clean at `-Wall -Wextra -Werror`. runtime/cell_rt.h section 7
// makes an `owned` callee responsible for the eventual free, and
// cell_slice_drop_glue frees the same BUFFER again when the box dies.
// cell_arc_clone increments a refcount, and the buffer is not what the
// refcount governs.
//
// R10 is refused at FOUR positions, not just this one: an `owned` parameter,
// an `owned` binding (`let owned ys: [Int] = xs`), an assignment into an
// `owned` place, and an `owned` struct field. The first three were measured as
// AddressSanitizer double frees; the fourth is not one only because this
// backend never drops a `record` shape, and becomes one when struct drops
// land.
//
// Every `arc`-to-`arc` use stays legal, which is what "the conversion is
// refused" has to be scoped to mean: the `arc` binding below builds the box,
// `let arc b = a` clones, an `arc` argument to an `arc` parameter clones, and
// an `arc` argument to a `shared` parameter borrows without cloning. Only
// making an `arc` place UNIQUE is refused.

pub fn take(owned xs: [Int]) -> Int;

pub fn main() {
    let arc xs = [1, 2, 3]
    let copy n = take(owned xs)
}
